## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests dynamic programming intuition on a constrained path counting problem with simple transitions and boundary conditions.

## Key Concepts
- **Recurrence relation:** f(i) = f(i+1) + f(i+2) with modulo arithmetic
- **Memoization vs. tabulation:** Trade-offs between recursive top-down with caching versus iterative bottom-up approaches
- **Edge conditions:** Blocked start/end positions, single-cell arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(blocked) {
  const n = blocked.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  // Memoized DFS
  const memo = Array(n).fill(-1);
  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n - 1) return blocked[i] ? 0 : 1;
    if (i >= n || blocked[i]) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    memo[i] = (dfs(i + 1) + dfs(i + 2)) % MOD;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up DP (O(1) space)
function countPathsDP(blocked) {
  const n = blocked.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  let next2 = 1, next1 = blocked[n - 1] ? 0 : 1;
  for (let i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
    const curr = blocked[i] ? 0 : (next1 + next2) % MOD;
    next2 = next1;
    next1 = curr;
  }
  return next1;
}
```

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

